#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Measure how standalone Unicode glyphs paint in this Ghostty + Nerd Font setup.

Covers UTF-8 1-, 2-, and 3-byte scalars (U+0000–U+FFFF). Combining marks,
controls, format chars, surrogates, and whitespace are skipped. Private Use
is included (Nerd Fonts live there).

What is measured
----------------
- **cells** — terminal occupancy from ``wcwidth`` (Ghostty's
  ``grapheme-width-method = unicode``).
- **class** — ``narrow`` (fits one cell), ``overlarge`` (occupies one cell but
  ink crosses into the next), ``wide`` (two-cell occupancy), ``missing``
  (assigned, but no ink / no glyph in the cascade).
- **density** — inked pixels / allocated-cell pixels, from a CoreText raster.
- **overflow_cells** — how far the ink bbox extends past the allocated cells,
  in units of one primary-font cell width.

The raster uses CoreText (Ghostty's engine on this Mac) at the configured
primary face plus the system fallback cascade. That is *not* 1:1 with
Ghostty's paint: Ghostty replaces box-drawing / block / braille / some
Powerline with internal sprites, and scales some Nerd icons. ``ghostty``
records ``+show-face`` when that tool is available.

Rasters (default on) are written to ``.rasters/{scale}x/{hi:02x}/{cp:04x}.png``.
Each PNG is 3×2 cells: half-cell pad left, origin cell, second cell, half-cell
pad right; half-cell pad above and below. Light 1px red lines mark the cell
boundaries. Density is measured on the allocated cell rectangle *before* the
grid is drawn.

Shape taxonomy is out of scope for this script.

Writes a TSV (the measurements), rasters, and hex-grid markdown tables.
"""

